xinput1_3: Let the first call return before the update thread touches windows.

The first XInput call starts the controller update thread and waits until it has
listed the controllers. The thread used to register its window class, create its
message window and register for device notifications first. On macOS that class
registration waited for the window lock, whose holder waited for the calling
thread: when the first call came from a game's main thread, the game hung for
good. Among Us (Unity, Rewired) froze on a black window right after its intro on
every graphics backend.

The thread now lists the controllers and lets the caller go before any window
work. A controller plugged in before the notification is registered is still
found by the thread's periodic rescan.

--- a/dlls/xinput1_3/main.c
+++ b/dlls/xinput1_3/main.c
@@ -719,14 +719,19 @@
 
     SetThreadDescription(GetCurrentThread(), L"wine_xinput_hid_update");
 
+    /* The caller that started this thread waits for the controller list, and may be a game's main thread that
+     * other threads need for window work. Let it go before taking any of the window lock ourselves: on macOS the
+     * class registration below waited for that lock while its holder waited for the game's main thread, and the
+     * game hung on its first XInput call. A controller plugged in before the notification is registered is found
+     * by the periodic rescan. */
+    update_controller_list();
+    SetEvent(start_event);
+
     RegisterClassExW(&cls);
     hwnd = CreateWindowExW(0, cls.lpszClassName, NULL, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,
                            HWND_MESSAGE, NULL, NULL, NULL);
     notif = RegisterDeviceNotificationW(hwnd, &filter, DEVICE_NOTIFY_WINDOW_HANDLE);
 
-    update_controller_list();
-    SetEvent(start_event);
-
     do
     {
         if (ret == count) while (PeekMessageW(&msg, hwnd, 0, 0, PM_REMOVE)) DispatchMessageW(&msg);
